(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("messaging",{"messaging/queues-logs-and-pub-sub-choosing-a-model":{"html":"\u003cp>Messaging is how services talk without waiting for each other. The three common shapes behave very differently under load, failure and change, so the first decision is the model, not the product.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Work queues, publish-subscribe and logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What each model keeps and for how long\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Questions that decide the model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where messaging helps and where it hurts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-shapes\">Three shapes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>work queue\u003c/strong> holds jobs. Each message goes to exactly one worker and is removed when handled. Use it to spread work (send an email, resize an image). \u003cstrong>Publish-subscribe\u003c/strong> copies each message to every subscriber, so one event reaches billing, search and analytics. A \u003cstrong>log\u003c/strong> appends messages in order and keeps them for a set time or size. Consumers remember their own position (offset), so they can read at different speeds, join later and replay.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Queue\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Pub/sub topic\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Log\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Delivery\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One consumer per message\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Every subscriber\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Every consumer group, by position\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>After reading\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Message removed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Usually removed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Kept until retention ends\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Replay\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Typical tools\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>RabbitMQ, SQS\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>SNS, Cloud Pub/Sub\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Kafka, Kinesis, Event Hubs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-use-messaging\">Why use messaging\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>It decouples senders from receivers in time (the receiver can be down), in speed (a buffer absorbs bursts) and in knowledge (the sender need not know who reads). The price is delay, more moving parts and new failure modes: duplicates, reordering, and a backlog that hides a failing consumer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"prod&gt;queue;prod&gt;topic;prod&gt;log;queue&gt;cons;topic&gt;cons;log&gt;cons\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Three messaging shapes\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prod\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Producer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"queue\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Work queue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"topic\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pub/sub topic\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"log\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Log with offsets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cons\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Consumers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Producer to Work queue; 2, Producer to Pub/sub topic; 3, Producer to Log with offsets; 4, Work queue to Consumers; 5, Pub/sub topic to Consumers; 6, Log with offsets to Consumers.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prod\">\u003cdt>Producer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sends messages without waiting for the reader.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it cannot reach the broker it needs a retry or buffer.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"queue\">\u003cdt>Work queue \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One message goes to one worker, then is removed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A growing queue means workers are too slow or failing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"topic\">\u003cdt>Pub/sub topic \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Copies each message to every subscriber.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A slow subscriber can lose messages if there is no buffer.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"log\">\u003cdt>Log with offsets \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Keeps ordered messages; readers track their position.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Retention too short means a late reader misses data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cons\">\u003cdt>Consumers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Process messages at their own pace.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A consumer that fails silently lets the backlog grow.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Ask: Does each message need handling once by one worker (queue)? Must several systems each see every message (topic or log)? Will anyone need to replay history, rebuild a view or fix a bug by reprocessing (log)? How many messages per second, and how long must they live? Small teams with modest volume are often well served by a managed queue; high-volume event streams and replay point to a log.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-data-that-could-not-be-replayed\">Incident: The data that could not be replayed\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A bug in a consumer corrupted a week of derived data, and the source messages were already deleted from the queue.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"rabbitmqctl list_queues name messages\npsql -c &quot;select count(*) from order_totals where total &lt; 0&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rabbitmqctl list_queues name messages\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">orders   0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select count(*) from order_totals where total &lt; 0&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">18422\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The consumer had acked every message, so the broker kept nothing. The team moved the stream to a log with seven days of retention, fixed the consumer, and reprocessed from the offset before the bug started.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing a product before choosing the model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a queue when many systems need the same events\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating a message broker as a database\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring what happens when the consumer is down for hours\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"three-shapes","text":"Three shapes"},{"id":"why-use-messaging","text":"Why use messaging"},{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},{"id":"incident-the-data-that-could-not-be-replayed","text":"Incident: The data that could not be replayed"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"RabbitMQ tutorials","u":"https://www.rabbitmq.com/tutorials"},{"t":"Apache Kafka introduction","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/intro"},{"t":"Amazon SQS developer guide","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/welcome.html"}],"text":"Messaging is how services talk without waiting for each other. The three common shapes behave very differently under load, failure and change, so the first decision is the model, not the product. What you will learn Work queues, publish-subscribe and logs What each model keeps and for how long Questions that decide the model Where messaging helps and where it hurts Three shapes A work queue holds jobs. Each message goes to exactly one worker and is removed when handled. Use it to spread work (send an email, resize an image). Publish-subscribe copies each message to every subscriber, so one event reaches billing, search and analytics. A log appends messages in order and keeps them for a set time or size. Consumers remember their own position (offset), so they can read at different speeds, join later and replay. Queue Pub/sub topic Log Delivery One consumer per message Every subscriber Every consumer group, by position After reading Message removed Usually removed Kept until retention ends Replay No No Yes Typical tools RabbitMQ, SQS SNS, Cloud Pub/Sub Kafka, Kinesis, Event Hubs Why use messaging It decouples senders from receivers in time (the receiver can be down), in speed (a buffer absorbs bursts) and in knowledge (the sender need not know who reads). The price is delay, more moving parts and new failure modes: duplicates, reordering, and a backlog that hides a failing consumer. Three messaging shapes Flow in order: 1, Producer to Work queue; 2, Producer to Pub/sub topic; 3, Producer to Log with offsets; 4, Work queue to Consumers; 5, Pub/sub topic to Consumers; 6, Log with offsets to Consumers. Step Queue Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Producer Step What it does: Sends messages without waiting for the reader. If it fails or is weak: If it cannot reach the broker it needs a retry or buffer. Work queue Queue What it does: One message goes to one worker, then is removed. If it fails or is weak: A growing queue means workers are too slow or failing. Pub/sub topic Queue What it does: Copies each message to every subscriber. If it fails or is weak: A slow subscriber can lose messages if there is no buffer. Log with offsets Queue What it does: Keeps ordered messages; readers track their position. If it fails or is weak: Retention too short means a late reader misses data. Consumers Step What it does: Process messages at their own pace. If it fails or is weak: A consumer that fails silently lets the backlog grow. Choosing Ask: Does each message need handling once by one worker (queue)? Must several systems each see every message (topic or log)? Will anyone need to replay history, rebuild a view or fix a bug by reprocessing (log)? How many messages per second, and how long must they live? Small teams with modest volume are often well served by a managed queue; high-volume event streams and replay point to a log. Incident: The data that could not be replayed Incident A bug in a consumer corrupted a week of derived data, and the source messages were already deleted from the queue. $ rabbitmqctl list_queues name messages orders 0 $ psql -c \"select count(*) from order_totals where total \u003c 0\" 18422 The consumer had acked every message, so the broker kept nothing. The team moved the stream to a log with seven days of retention, fixed the consumer, and reprocessed from the offset before the bug started. Common mistakes Choosing a product before choosing the model Using a queue when many systems need the same events Treating a message broker as a database Ignoring what happens when the consumer is down for hours"},"messaging/delivery-guarantees-ordering-and-idempotency":{"html":"\u003cp>The hardest promises in messaging are about what happens on failure. A sender cannot know whether a message arrived if the acknowledgement was lost, so it must choose between sending again and risking a duplicate, or not sending and risking a loss.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>At-most-once, at-least-once and exactly-once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where ordering is guaranteed and where it is not\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Idempotent consumers and idempotency keys\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The outbox pattern for sending messages reliably\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-three-promises\">The three promises\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>At-most-once:\u003c/strong> send and forget. No duplicates, but a lost message is gone.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>At-least-once:\u003c/strong> retry until acknowledged. No loss, but duplicates happen after a lost acknowledgement or a consumer crash.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Exactly-once:\u003c/strong> each message has one effect. Brokers can offer it within their own system (Kafka transactions), but across a database and a broker it is achieved by making the \u003cstrong>effect\u003c/strong> idempotent.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Most production systems run at-least-once and make handlers safe to repeat.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ordering\">Ordering\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Messages are ordered only within a single queue or \u003cstrong>partition\u003c/strong>. Two consumers on one queue may finish out of order, and retries reorder messages. To keep related messages in order, send them with the same \u003cstrong>key\u003c/strong> (an order id) so they land in the same partition, and process each partition sequentially.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"idempotent-consumers\">Idempotent consumers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"-- processed_messages(message_id primary key)\nbegin;\ninsert into processed_messages(message_id) values ('evt-7f3a')\n  on conflict do nothing;\n-- if 0 rows were inserted, the message was already handled: skip\nupdate accounts set balance = balance - 20 where id = 42;\ncommit;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- processed_messages(message_id primary key)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">begin\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">insert into\u003c/span> processed_messages(message_id) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">values\u003c/span> (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;evt-7f3a&#x27;\u003c/span>)\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">on\u003c/span> conflict do nothing;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">-- if 0 rows were inserted, the message was already handled: skip\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">update\u003c/span> accounts \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">set\u003c/span> balance \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> balance \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">where\u003c/span> id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">42\u003c/span>;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">commit\u003c/span>;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Store the message id and the effect in one transaction. Or use naturally idempotent operations: \u003ccode>set status = &#39;shipped&#39;\u003c/code> is safe to repeat, \u003ccode>balance = balance - 20\u003c/code> is not without a guard.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-outbox-pattern\">The outbox pattern\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Writing to the database and publishing to a broker are two actions that can half-fail. Write the event to an \u003cstrong>outbox table\u003c/strong> in the same transaction as the data change; a separate relay reads the table and publishes, retrying until the broker confirms.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"svc&gt;out;out&gt;relay;relay&gt;broker;broker&gt;cons\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Safe sending and receiving\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svc\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"out\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Outbox table\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"relay\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Relay\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"broker\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Broker\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cons\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Idempotent consumer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Service to Outbox table; 2, Outbox table to Relay; 3, Relay to Broker; 4, Broker to Idempotent consumer.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svc\">\u003cdt>Service \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Changes data and writes an outbox row in one transaction.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Publishing directly can lose the event if the process dies between steps.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"out\">\u003cdt>Outbox table \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds events until they are published.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without cleanup it grows forever.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"relay\">\u003cdt>Relay \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Publishes outbox rows to the broker and retries.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Retries cause duplicates, which the consumer must handle.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"broker\">\u003cdt>Broker \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Delivers at least once to consumers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An acknowledgement lost on the way causes redelivery.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cons\">\u003cdt>Idempotent consumer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Checks the message id before applying the effect.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without the check a redelivery repeats the effect.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-charged-twice\">Incident: Charged twice\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A payment consumer crashed after charging but before it acknowledged the message, so the broker redelivered it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">payments.log: charge ok order=4411 amount=49.00\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">payments.log: consumer restarted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">payments.log: charge ok order=4411 amount=49.00\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">support: 1 customer charged twice\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The consumer had no record of what it had handled. The team added an idempotency key (the order id) to the payment call and a processed table, then refunded the duplicates found by a one-off query.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming exactly-once because the broker advertises it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Acknowledging a message before the work is saved\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Relying on global ordering across partitions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Publishing straight after a database commit with no outbox\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-three-promises","text":"The three promises"},{"id":"ordering","text":"Ordering"},{"id":"idempotent-consumers","text":"Idempotent consumers"},{"id":"the-outbox-pattern","text":"The outbox pattern"},{"id":"incident-charged-twice","text":"Incident: Charged twice"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kafka delivery semantics","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#semantics"},{"t":"RabbitMQ reliability guide","u":"https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/reliability"}],"text":"The hardest promises in messaging are about what happens on failure. A sender cannot know whether a message arrived if the acknowledgement was lost, so it must choose between sending again and risking a duplicate, or not sending and risking a loss. What you will learn At-most-once, at-least-once and exactly-once Where ordering is guaranteed and where it is not Idempotent consumers and idempotency keys The outbox pattern for sending messages reliably The three promises At-most-once: send and forget. No duplicates, but a lost message is gone. At-least-once: retry until acknowledged. No loss, but duplicates happen after a lost acknowledgement or a consumer crash. Exactly-once: each message has one effect. Brokers can offer it within their own system (Kafka transactions), but across a database and a broker it is achieved by making the effect idempotent. Most production systems run at-least-once and make handlers safe to repeat. Ordering Messages are ordered only within a single queue or partition . Two consumers on one queue may finish out of order, and retries reorder messages. To keep related messages in order, send them with the same key (an order id) so they land in the same partition, and process each partition sequentially. Idempotent consumers -- processed_messages(message_id primary key) begin ; insert into processed_messages(message_id) values ( &#x27;evt-7f3a&#x27; ) on conflict do nothing; -- if 0 rows were inserted, the message was already handled: skip update accounts set balance = balance - 20 where id = 42 ; commit ; Store the message id and the effect in one transaction. Or use naturally idempotent operations: set status = &#39;shipped&#39; is safe to repeat, balance = balance - 20 is not without a guard. The outbox pattern Writing to the database and publishing to a broker are two actions that can half-fail. Write the event to an outbox table in the same transaction as the data change; a separate relay reads the table and publishes, retrying until the broker confirms. Safe sending and receiving Flow in order: 1, Service to Outbox table; 2, Outbox table to Relay; 3, Relay to Broker; 4, Broker to Idempotent consumer. Service Data store Step Queue Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Service Service What it does: Changes data and writes an outbox row in one transaction. If it fails or is weak: Publishing directly can lose the event if the process dies between steps. Outbox table Data store What it does: Holds events until they are published. If it fails or is weak: Without cleanup it grows forever. Relay Step What it does: Publishes outbox rows to the broker and retries. If it fails or is weak: Retries cause duplicates, which the consumer must handle. Broker Queue What it does: Delivers at least once to consumers. If it fails or is weak: An acknowledgement lost on the way causes redelivery. Idempotent consumer Security What it does: Checks the message id before applying the effect. If it fails or is weak: Without the check a redelivery repeats the effect. Incident: Charged twice Incident A payment consumer crashed after charging but before it acknowledged the message, so the broker redelivered it. payments.log: charge ok order=4411 amount=49.00 payments.log: consumer restarted payments.log: charge ok order=4411 amount=49.00 support: 1 customer charged twice The consumer had no record of what it had handled. The team added an idempotency key (the order id) to the payment call and a processed table, then refunded the duplicates found by a one-off query. Common mistakes Assuming exactly-once because the broker advertises it Acknowledging a message before the work is saved Relying on global ordering across partitions Publishing straight after a database commit with no outbox"},"messaging/rabbitmq-exchanges-queues-and-routing":{"html":"\u003cp>RabbitMQ is a widely used message broker built on AMQP. Its power is in routing: the producer does not know which queues exist, only the exchange and a routing key. Learning that one layer of indirection makes the rest of RabbitMQ easy to reason about.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Producers, exchanges, bindings and queues\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Direct, topic, fanout and headers exchanges\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Durability and persistence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Virtual hosts, users and the management interface\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-model\">The model\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>producer\u003c/strong> publishes a message to an \u003cstrong>exchange\u003c/strong> with a \u003cstrong>routing key\u003c/strong>. The exchange applies its rules to \u003cstrong>bindings\u003c/strong> (links from the exchange to queues) and copies the message into each matching \u003cstrong>queue\u003c/strong>. \u003cstrong>Consumers\u003c/strong> read from queues.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Exchange\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Routes by\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>direct\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Exact match of routing key to binding key\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>topic\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pattern match: \u003ccode>orders.*.eu\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>#\u003c/code> for everything\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>fanout\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Every bound queue, ignoring the key\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>headers\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Message header values\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"rabbitmqadmin declare exchange name=events type=topic durable=true\nrabbitmqadmin declare queue name=billing durable=true\nrabbitmqadmin declare binding source=events destination=billing routing_key='order.paid.#'\nrabbitmqadmin publish exchange=events routing_key=order.paid.eu payload='{&quot;id&quot;:4411}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rabbitmqadmin \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">declare\u003c/span> exchange name=events \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=topic durable=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rabbitmqadmin \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">declare\u003c/span> queue name=billing durable=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rabbitmqadmin \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">declare\u003c/span> binding \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">source\u003c/span>=events destination=billing routing_key=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;order.paid.#&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rabbitmqadmin publish exchange=events routing_key=order.paid.eu payload=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;id&quot;:4411}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"durability\">Durability\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Messages survive a broker restart only if the \u003cstrong>queue is durable\u003c/strong> and the \u003cstrong>message is persistent\u003c/strong>. For stronger safety use \u003cstrong>quorum queues\u003c/strong>, which replicate across nodes using a consensus protocol and are the recommended type for important data. Classic non-replicated queues lose messages if their node is lost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"virtual-hosts-and-access\">Virtual hosts and access\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>vhost\u003c/strong> is an isolated namespace of exchanges, queues and permissions. Use one per environment or application. Give each service its own user with permissions only on its vhost. The management UI (port 15672) and \u003ccode>rabbitmqctl list_queues\u003c/code> show message counts, consumers and rates.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"prod&gt;ex;ex&gt;q1;ex&gt;q2;q1&gt;cons\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How RabbitMQ routes a message\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prod\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Producer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ex\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Exchange\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"q1\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Billing queue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"q2\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Search queue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cons\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Consumers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Producer to Exchange; 2, Exchange to Billing queue; 3, Exchange to Search queue; 4, Billing queue to Consumers.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prod\">\u003cdt>Producer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Publishes to an exchange with a routing key.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Publishing to a missing exchange fails or the message is dropped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ex\">\u003cdt>Exchange \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Applies routing rules to decide which queues get a copy.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong binding silently drops messages that match nothing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"q1\">\u003cdt>Billing queue \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Bound with order.paid.# and read by billing.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not durable means messages vanish on restart.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"q2\">\u003cdt>Search queue \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Bound to the same key for another consumer.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A slow consumer fills memory and disk.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cons\">\u003cdt>Consumers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Read, process and acknowledge messages.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No consumer means the queue only grows.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-messages-that-matched-nothing\">Incident: The messages that matched nothing\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new routing key had a typo and an exchange dropped events with no error.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"rabbitmqctl list_exchanges name type\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rabbitmqctl list_exchanges name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">events topic\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">management: events unroutable_dropped 3210\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">binding: events -&gt; billing  (order.paid.#)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The producer published order.payed.eu, which matched no binding, so the exchange dropped the messages. The team turned on the mandatory flag and an alternate exchange for unroutable messages, and added an alert on the dropped count.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Publishing to the default exchange with a queue name everywhere\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Non-durable queues for important work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One shared user and vhost for every application\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert for unroutable messages\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-model","text":"The model"},{"id":"durability","text":"Durability"},{"id":"virtual-hosts-and-access","text":"Virtual hosts and access"},{"id":"incident-the-messages-that-matched-nothing","text":"Incident: The messages that matched nothing"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"RabbitMQ concepts","u":"https://www.rabbitmq.com/tutorials/amqp-concepts"},{"t":"RabbitMQ exchanges","u":"https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/exchanges"}],"text":"RabbitMQ is a widely used message broker built on AMQP. Its power is in routing: the producer does not know which queues exist, only the exchange and a routing key. Learning that one layer of indirection makes the rest of RabbitMQ easy to reason about. What you will learn Producers, exchanges, bindings and queues Direct, topic, fanout and headers exchanges Durability and persistence Virtual hosts, users and the management interface The model A producer publishes a message to an exchange with a routing key . The exchange applies its rules to bindings (links from the exchange to queues) and copies the message into each matching queue . Consumers read from queues. Exchange Routes by direct Exact match of routing key to binding key topic Pattern match: orders.*.eu , # for everything fanout Every bound queue, ignoring the key headers Message header values $ rabbitmqadmin declare exchange name=events type =topic durable= true $ rabbitmqadmin declare queue name=billing durable= true $ rabbitmqadmin declare binding source =events destination=billing routing_key= &#x27;order.paid.#&#x27; $ rabbitmqadmin publish exchange=events routing_key=order.paid.eu payload= &#x27;{\"id\":4411}&#x27; Durability Messages survive a broker restart only if the queue is durable and the message is persistent . For stronger safety use quorum queues , which replicate across nodes using a consensus protocol and are the recommended type for important data. Classic non-replicated queues lose messages if their node is lost. Virtual hosts and access A vhost is an isolated namespace of exchanges, queues and permissions. Use one per environment or application. Give each service its own user with permissions only on its vhost. The management UI (port 15672) and rabbitmqctl list_queues show message counts, consumers and rates. How RabbitMQ routes a message Flow in order: 1, Producer to Exchange; 2, Exchange to Billing queue; 3, Exchange to Search queue; 4, Billing queue to Consumers. Step Queue Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Producer Step What it does: Publishes to an exchange with a routing key. If it fails or is weak: Publishing to a missing exchange fails or the message is dropped. Exchange Step What it does: Applies routing rules to decide which queues get a copy. If it fails or is weak: A wrong binding silently drops messages that match nothing. Billing queue Queue What it does: Bound with order.paid.# and read by billing. If it fails or is weak: Not durable means messages vanish on restart. Search queue Queue What it does: Bound to the same key for another consumer. If it fails or is weak: A slow consumer fills memory and disk. Consumers Step What it does: Read, process and acknowledge messages. If it fails or is weak: No consumer means the queue only grows. Incident: The messages that matched nothing Incident A new routing key had a typo and an exchange dropped events with no error. $ rabbitmqctl list_exchanges name type events topic management: events unroutable_dropped 3210 binding: events -> billing (order.paid.#) The producer published order.payed.eu, which matched no binding, so the exchange dropped the messages. The team turned on the mandatory flag and an alternate exchange for unroutable messages, and added an alert on the dropped count. Common mistakes Publishing to the default exchange with a queue name everywhere Non-durable queues for important work One shared user and vhost for every application No alert for unroutable messages"},"messaging/rabbitmq-acknowledgements-prefetch-and-dead-letters":{"html":"\u003cp>Most RabbitMQ incidents are about consumers: not acknowledging, taking too much, or retrying a bad message forever. Three settings and one pattern, acknowledgements, prefetch, TTL and dead-lettering, prevent most of them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Manual acknowledgements and publisher confirms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prefetch and fair dispatch\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dead-letter exchanges and retry delays\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading ready, unacked and consumer numbers\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"acknowledgements\">Acknowledgements\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With \u003cstrong>manual acks\u003c/strong> the broker keeps a message as \u003cstrong>unacked\u003c/strong> until the consumer confirms it. If the connection drops, the message returns to the queue and is delivered again (at-least-once). Acknowledge only after the work is saved. On the producer side, \u003cstrong>publisher confirms\u003c/strong> tell the producer the broker has safely taken the message.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prefetch\">Prefetch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"channel.basic_qos(prefetch_count=20)     # at most 20 unacked per consumer\nchannel.basic_consume(queue='billing', on_message_callback=handle, auto_ack=False)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">channel.basic_qos(prefetch_count=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>)     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># at most 20 unacked per consumer\u003c/span>\nchannel.basic_consume(queue=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;billing&#x27;\u003c/span>, on_message_callback=handle, auto_ack=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Without a limit one consumer can receive thousands of messages and sit on them while others are idle. A sensible prefetch (10 to 100 for quick tasks, 1 for slow ones) spreads work fairly and bounds memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dead-letters-and-retries\">Dead letters and retries\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"rabbitmqadmin declare queue name=billing durable=true \\\n    arguments='{&quot;x-dead-letter-exchange&quot;:&quot;dlx&quot;,&quot;x-message-ttl&quot;:600000}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rabbitmqadmin \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">declare\u003c/span> queue name=billing durable=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span> \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    arguments=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;x-dead-letter-exchange&quot;:&quot;dlx&quot;,&quot;x-message-ttl&quot;:600000}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>When a message is rejected without requeue, expires, or the queue overflows, the broker republishes it to the \u003cstrong>dead-letter exchange\u003c/strong>. A common design: a \u003ccode>retry\u003c/code> queue with a TTL (say 30 s) whose dead-letter target is the original queue, giving a delayed retry; after N attempts (counted in a header) the message goes to a \u003ccode>parked\u003c/code> queue for humans.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-watch\">What to watch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Metric\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Worry when\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Ready\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Waiting for a consumer\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Growing steadily\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Unacked\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Delivered, not yet acknowledged\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Large and stuck\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Consumers\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Connected consumers\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Zero\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Publish vs ack rate\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>In versus out\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Publish far above ack\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"q&gt;c;c&gt;retry;retry&gt;q;retry&gt;dead;dead&gt;mon\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Failure handling in RabbitMQ\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"12.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"q\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Main queue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"c\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Consumer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"retry\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Retry queue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dead\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Parked queue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mon\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Alerts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Main queue to Consumer; 2, Consumer to Retry queue; 3, Retry queue to Main queue; 4, Retry queue to Parked queue; 5, Parked queue to Alerts.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"q\">\u003cdt>Main queue \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Delivers up to prefetch messages to each consumer.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without prefetch one consumer can hoard messages.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"c\">\u003cdt>Consumer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Processes then acknowledges, or rejects without requeue.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Acking too early loses work if it crashes next.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"retry\">\u003cdt>Retry queue \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds a failed message for a TTL, then returns it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unlimited retries loop forever on a poison message.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dead\">\u003cdt>Parked queue \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Keeps messages that failed N times for a human.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Nobody watching it means silent data loss.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mon\">\u003cdt>Alerts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Watch ready, unacked and parked counts.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An alert on only queue length misses a stuck consumer.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-poison-message\">Incident: The poison message\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>One malformed message crashed its consumer, was requeued at once and blocked the queue.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"rabbitmqctl list_queues name messages_ready messages_unacknowledged consumers\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rabbitmqctl list_queues name messages_ready messages_unacknowledged consumers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">orders  52113  1  1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">consumer log: JSONDecodeError at offset 0 (restart 480)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The bad message was rejected with requeue true, so it came back first every time. The fix was reject without requeue to a dead-letter queue, plus a parked queue alert, so the 52,000 good messages could be processed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using auto-ack for work that must not be lost\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rejecting with requeue true for messages that can never succeed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No prefetch limit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A dead-letter queue with no owner or alert\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"acknowledgements","text":"Acknowledgements"},{"id":"prefetch","text":"Prefetch"},{"id":"dead-letters-and-retries","text":"Dead letters and retries"},{"id":"what-to-watch","text":"What to watch"},{"id":"incident-the-poison-message","text":"Incident: The poison message"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Consumer acknowledgements and publisher confirms","u":"https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/confirms"},{"t":"Dead letter exchanges","u":"https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/dlx"}],"text":"Most RabbitMQ incidents are about consumers: not acknowledging, taking too much, or retrying a bad message forever. Three settings and one pattern, acknowledgements, prefetch, TTL and dead-lettering, prevent most of them. What you will learn Manual acknowledgements and publisher confirms Prefetch and fair dispatch Dead-letter exchanges and retry delays Reading ready, unacked and consumer numbers Acknowledgements With manual acks the broker keeps a message as unacked until the consumer confirms it. If the connection drops, the message returns to the queue and is delivered again (at-least-once). Acknowledge only after the work is saved. On the producer side, publisher confirms tell the producer the broker has safely taken the message. Prefetch channel.basic_qos(prefetch_count= 20 ) # at most 20 unacked per consumer channel.basic_consume(queue= &#x27;billing&#x27; , on_message_callback=handle, auto_ack= False ) Without a limit one consumer can receive thousands of messages and sit on them while others are idle. A sensible prefetch (10 to 100 for quick tasks, 1 for slow ones) spreads work fairly and bounds memory. Dead letters and retries $ rabbitmqadmin declare queue name=billing durable= true \\ arguments= &#x27;{\"x-dead-letter-exchange\":\"dlx\",\"x-message-ttl\":600000}&#x27; When a message is rejected without requeue, expires, or the queue overflows, the broker republishes it to the dead-letter exchange . A common design: a retry queue with a TTL (say 30 s) whose dead-letter target is the original queue, giving a delayed retry; after N attempts (counted in a header) the message goes to a parked queue for humans. What to watch Metric Meaning Worry when Ready Waiting for a consumer Growing steadily Unacked Delivered, not yet acknowledged Large and stuck Consumers Connected consumers Zero Publish vs ack rate In versus out Publish far above ack Failure handling in RabbitMQ Flow in order: 1, Main queue to Consumer; 2, Consumer to Retry queue; 3, Retry queue to Main queue; 4, Retry queue to Parked queue; 5, Parked queue to Alerts. Queue Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Main queue Queue What it does: Delivers up to prefetch messages to each consumer. If it fails or is weak: Without prefetch one consumer can hoard messages. Consumer Step What it does: Processes then acknowledges, or rejects without requeue. If it fails or is weak: Acking too early loses work if it crashes next. Retry queue Queue What it does: Holds a failed message for a TTL, then returns it. If it fails or is weak: Unlimited retries loop forever on a poison message. Parked queue Queue What it does: Keeps messages that failed N times for a human. If it fails or is weak: Nobody watching it means silent data loss. Alerts Observability What it does: Watch ready, unacked and parked counts. If it fails or is weak: An alert on only queue length misses a stuck consumer. Incident: The poison message Incident One malformed message crashed its consumer, was requeued at once and blocked the queue. $ rabbitmqctl list_queues name messages_ready messages_unacknowledged consumers orders 52113 1 1 consumer log: JSONDecodeError at offset 0 (restart 480) The bad message was rejected with requeue true, so it came back first every time. The fix was reject without requeue to a dead-letter queue, plus a parked queue alert, so the 52,000 good messages could be processed. Common mistakes Using auto-ack for work that must not be lost Rejecting with requeue true for messages that can never succeed No prefetch limit A dead-letter queue with no owner or alert"},"messaging/kafka-topics-partitions-and-brokers":{"html":"\u003cp>Kafka is a distributed commit log. Producers append records, consumers read at their own pace, and the data stays for a chosen time. Everything about scale and ordering comes from one idea, the partition, so it is the first thing to understand.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Topics, partitions, offsets and records\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Brokers, replicas and the controller\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keys and how they choose a partition\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing a partition count\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"topics-and-partitions\">Topics and partitions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>topic\u003c/strong> is a named stream. It is divided into \u003cstrong>partitions\u003c/strong>; each partition is an ordered, append-only file split into segments on disk. Each record in a partition gets an \u003cstrong>offset\u003c/strong>: 0, 1, 2 and so on. There is no global order across partitions, only within one.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --create --topic orders --partitions 6 --replication-factor 3\nkafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --describe --topic orders\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --create --topic orders --partitions 6 --replication-factor 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --describe --topic orders\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Topic: orders  PartitionCount: 6  ReplicationFactor: 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Partition: 0  Leader: 1  Replicas: 1,2,3  Isr: 1,2,3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Partition: 1  Leader: 2  Replicas: 2,3,1  Isr: 2,3,1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"brokers-and-replicas\">Brokers and replicas\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>broker\u003c/strong> is a Kafka server. Each partition has one \u003cstrong>leader\u003c/strong> broker that handles reads and writes, and \u003cstrong>followers\u003c/strong> that copy it. The set of followers that are caught up is the \u003cstrong>ISR\u003c/strong> (in-sync replicas). Modern clusters run in \u003cstrong>KRaft\u003c/strong> mode, where Kafka brokers manage cluster metadata themselves with a Raft quorum instead of using ZooKeeper.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keys\">Keys\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A producer may attach a \u003cstrong>key\u003c/strong>. Records with the same key hash to the same partition, so they stay in order. With no key, records are spread across partitions. Use the entity id (order id, user id) as the key when order per entity matters.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-many-partitions\">How many partitions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Partitions set the maximum parallelism of a \u003cstrong>consumer group\u003c/strong>: with 6 partitions at most 6 consumers read in parallel. More partitions give more throughput and more files, open handles and recovery time. Adding partitions later is possible but changes which partition a key maps to; reducing is not supported. Plan for the growth you expect, then add headroom.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"prod&gt;b1;prod&gt;b2;b1&gt;b3;b2&gt;cons\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A Kafka topic across brokers\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prod\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Producers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"b1\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Broker 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"b2\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Broker 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"b3\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Broker 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cons\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Consumer group\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Producers to Broker 1; 2, Producers to Broker 2; 3, Broker 1 to Broker 3; 4, Broker 2 to Consumer group.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prod\">\u003cdt>Producers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Append records, choosing a partition by key.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A hot key sends all load to one partition.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"b1\">\u003cdt>Broker 1 \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Leader for some partitions, follower for others.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Losing it moves leadership to followers in the ISR.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"b2\">\u003cdt>Broker 2 \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds replicas and leaders too.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A slow disk here slows its leader partitions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"b3\">\u003cdt>Broker 3 \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Third copy for fault tolerance.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Two brokers down with replication 3 can stop writes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cons\">\u003cdt>Consumer group \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Each partition is read by one consumer in the group.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> More consumers than partitions leaves some idle.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-three-partitions-twenty-consumers\">Incident: Three partitions, twenty consumers\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A service needed to scale to twenty consumers but the topic had only three partitions.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --describe --group invoicer\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --describe --group invoicer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">GROUP     TOPIC   PARTITION  LAG\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">invoicer  orders  0          1,401,000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">invoicer  orders  1          1,380,000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">invoicer  orders  2          1,352,000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">17 members have no partition assigned\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Adding consumers did nothing. The team created a new topic with 24 partitions, copied the old data and cut over producers, because adding partitions to the old topic would have broken key ordering for existing data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing too few partitions to save effort\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a low-cardinality key that makes one hot partition\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting order across partitions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding partitions without understanding the key mapping change\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"topics-and-partitions","text":"Topics and partitions"},{"id":"brokers-and-replicas","text":"Brokers and replicas"},{"id":"keys","text":"Keys"},{"id":"how-many-partitions","text":"How many partitions"},{"id":"incident-three-partitions-twenty-consumers","text":"Incident: Three partitions, twenty consumers"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kafka documentation: design","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#design"},{"t":"Kafka introduction","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/intro"}],"text":"Kafka is a distributed commit log. Producers append records, consumers read at their own pace, and the data stays for a chosen time. Everything about scale and ordering comes from one idea, the partition, so it is the first thing to understand. What you will learn Topics, partitions, offsets and records Brokers, replicas and the controller Keys and how they choose a partition Choosing a partition count Topics and partitions A topic is a named stream. It is divided into partitions ; each partition is an ordered, append-only file split into segments on disk. Each record in a partition gets an offset : 0, 1, 2 and so on. There is no global order across partitions, only within one. $ kafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --create --topic orders --partitions 6 --replication-factor 3 $ kafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --describe --topic orders Topic: orders PartitionCount: 6 ReplicationFactor: 3 Partition: 0 Leader: 1 Replicas: 1,2,3 Isr: 1,2,3 Partition: 1 Leader: 2 Replicas: 2,3,1 Isr: 2,3,1 Brokers and replicas A broker is a Kafka server. Each partition has one leader broker that handles reads and writes, and followers that copy it. The set of followers that are caught up is the ISR (in-sync replicas). Modern clusters run in KRaft mode, where Kafka brokers manage cluster metadata themselves with a Raft quorum instead of using ZooKeeper. Keys A producer may attach a key . Records with the same key hash to the same partition, so they stay in order. With no key, records are spread across partitions. Use the entity id (order id, user id) as the key when order per entity matters. How many partitions Partitions set the maximum parallelism of a consumer group : with 6 partitions at most 6 consumers read in parallel. More partitions give more throughput and more files, open handles and recovery time. Adding partitions later is possible but changes which partition a key maps to; reducing is not supported. Plan for the growth you expect, then add headroom. A Kafka topic across brokers Flow in order: 1, Producers to Broker 1; 2, Producers to Broker 2; 3, Broker 1 to Broker 3; 4, Broker 2 to Consumer group. Step Queue Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Producers Step What it does: Append records, choosing a partition by key. If it fails or is weak: A hot key sends all load to one partition. Broker 1 Queue What it does: Leader for some partitions, follower for others. If it fails or is weak: Losing it moves leadership to followers in the ISR. Broker 2 Data store What it does: Holds replicas and leaders too. If it fails or is weak: A slow disk here slows its leader partitions. Broker 3 Data store What it does: Third copy for fault tolerance. If it fails or is weak: Two brokers down with replication 3 can stop writes. Consumer group Step What it does: Each partition is read by one consumer in the group. If it fails or is weak: More consumers than partitions leaves some idle. Incident: Three partitions, twenty consumers Incident A service needed to scale to twenty consumers but the topic had only three partitions. $ kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --describe --group invoicer GROUP TOPIC PARTITION LAG invoicer orders 0 1,401,000 invoicer orders 1 1,380,000 invoicer orders 2 1,352,000 17 members have no partition assigned Adding consumers did nothing. The team created a new topic with 24 partitions, copied the old data and cut over producers, because adding partitions to the old topic would have broken key ordering for existing data. Common mistakes Choosing too few partitions to save effort Using a low-cardinality key that makes one hot partition Expecting order across partitions Adding partitions without understanding the key mapping change"},"messaging/kafka-producers-consumers-and-consumer-groups":{"html":"\u003cp>Kafka clients carry much of the responsibility. A producer decides how durable a write must be; a consumer decides when its progress is saved. Wrong defaults in either quietly lose or repeat data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Producer acknowledgements, idempotence and batching\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The consumer poll loop and offset commits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Consumer groups, assignment and rebalances\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Settings that reduce rebalance trouble\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"producer-settings\">Producer settings\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"properties\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"acks=all                    # wait for all in-sync replicas\nenable.idempotence=true     # no duplicates from producer retries\nretries=2147483647\ndelivery.timeout.ms=120000\nlinger.ms=10                # wait a little to build bigger batches\nbatch.size=65536\ncompression.type=lz4\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">acks\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">all                    # wait for all in-sync replicas\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">enable.idempotence\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">true     # no duplicates from producer retries\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">retries\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">2147483647\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">delivery.timeout.ms\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">120000\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">linger.ms\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">10                # wait a little to build bigger batches\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">batch.size\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">65536\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">compression.type\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">lz4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>acks=all\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>min.insync.replicas=2\u003c/code> means a record is acknowledged only after at least two replicas have it. \u003ccode>enable.idempotence\u003c/code> lets the broker discard duplicates caused by retries. Batching and compression raise throughput at the cost of a few milliseconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-consumer-loop\">The consumer loop\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"consumer = KafkaConsumer('orders', group_id='invoicer', enable_auto_commit=False)\nfor batch in consumer:\n    handle(batch)            # do the work first\n    consumer.commit()        # then record progress\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">consumer = KafkaConsumer(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;orders&#x27;\u003c/span>, group_id=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;invoicer&#x27;\u003c/span>, enable_auto_commit=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> batch \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> consumer:\n    handle(batch)            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># do the work first\u003c/span>\n    consumer.commit()        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># then record progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Auto-commit can commit offsets for records you have not finished. Commit after processing for \u003cstrong>at-least-once\u003c/strong>; make \u003ccode>handle\u003c/code> idempotent so a replay after a crash is safe.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"consumer-groups-and-rebalances\">Consumer groups and rebalances\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>All consumers with the same \u003ccode>group.id\u003c/code> share a topic: a \u003cstrong>group coordinator\u003c/strong> broker assigns each partition to one member. When a member joins, leaves or stops sending heartbeats, the group \u003cstrong>rebalances\u003c/strong> and partitions move. During a classic rebalance, consumption pauses. Reduce the pain with \u003ccode>session.timeout.ms\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>max.poll.interval.ms\u003c/code> set to match real processing time, cooperative rebalancing, and \u003cstrong>static membership\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>group.instance.id\u003c/code>) so a quick restart does not trigger a rebalance.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"coord&gt;c1;coord&gt;c2;topic&gt;c1;topic&gt;c2;c1&gt;off\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A consumer group sharing partitions\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"83.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"topic\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Topic with 6 partitions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"coord\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Group coordinator\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"c1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Consumer 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"c2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Consumer 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"off\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Committed offsets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Group coordinator to Consumer 1; 2, Group coordinator to Consumer 2; 3, Topic with 6 partitions to Consumer 1; 4, Topic with 6 partitions to Consumer 2; 5, Consumer 1 to Committed offsets.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"topic\">\u003cdt>Topic with 6 partitions \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The data to be read.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A hot partition makes one consumer slower than the rest.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"coord\">\u003cdt>Group coordinator \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Assigns partitions and tracks heartbeats.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it is slow, rebalances take longer.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"c1\">\u003cdt>Consumer 1 \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reads partitions 0 to 2.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it stalls, the group sees lag on its partitions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"c2\">\u003cdt>Consumer 2 \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reads partitions 3 to 5.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A crash triggers a rebalance for the whole group.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"off\">\u003cdt>Committed offsets \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The saved position for each partition.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Committing early loses records; late repeats them.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-committed-before-it-was-done\">Incident: Committed before it was done\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Offsets were auto-committed while a batch was still processing, and a crash lost 800 records.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe --group invoicer\npsql -c &quot;select count(*) from invoices&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe --group invoicer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">orders 0  CURRENT-OFFSET 5000  LOG-END-OFFSET 5000  LAG 0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select count(*) from invoices&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">4200\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Lag showed zero, which looked healthy, but 800 records had been marked as read and never processed. The team moved to manual commits after the write and added a count check between topic and table.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Leaving enable.auto.commit on with slow processing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>acks=1 for data that must not be lost\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A poll interval shorter than the processing time, causing endless rebalances\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running more consumers than partitions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"producer-settings","text":"Producer settings"},{"id":"the-consumer-loop","text":"The consumer loop"},{"id":"consumer-groups-and-rebalances","text":"Consumer groups and rebalances"},{"id":"incident-committed-before-it-was-done","text":"Incident: Committed before it was done"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kafka producer configs","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#producerconfigs"},{"t":"Kafka consumer configs","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#consumerconfigs"}],"text":"Kafka clients carry much of the responsibility. A producer decides how durable a write must be; a consumer decides when its progress is saved. Wrong defaults in either quietly lose or repeat data. What you will learn Producer acknowledgements, idempotence and batching The consumer poll loop and offset commits Consumer groups, assignment and rebalances Settings that reduce rebalance trouble Producer settings acks = all # wait for all in-sync replicas enable.idempotence = true # no duplicates from producer retries retries = 2147483647 delivery.timeout.ms = 120000 linger.ms = 10 # wait a little to build bigger batches batch.size = 65536 compression.type = lz4 acks=all with min.insync.replicas=2 means a record is acknowledged only after at least two replicas have it. enable.idempotence lets the broker discard duplicates caused by retries. Batching and compression raise throughput at the cost of a few milliseconds. The consumer loop consumer = KafkaConsumer( &#x27;orders&#x27; , group_id= &#x27;invoicer&#x27; , enable_auto_commit= False ) for batch in consumer: handle(batch) # do the work first consumer.commit() # then record progress Auto-commit can commit offsets for records you have not finished. Commit after processing for at-least-once ; make handle idempotent so a replay after a crash is safe. Consumer groups and rebalances All consumers with the same group.id share a topic: a group coordinator broker assigns each partition to one member. When a member joins, leaves or stops sending heartbeats, the group rebalances and partitions move. During a classic rebalance, consumption pauses. Reduce the pain with session.timeout.ms and max.poll.interval.ms set to match real processing time, cooperative rebalancing, and static membership ( group.instance.id ) so a quick restart does not trigger a rebalance. A consumer group sharing partitions Flow in order: 1, Group coordinator to Consumer 1; 2, Group coordinator to Consumer 2; 3, Topic with 6 partitions to Consumer 1; 4, Topic with 6 partitions to Consumer 2; 5, Consumer 1 to Committed offsets. Queue Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Topic with 6 partitions Queue What it does: The data to be read. If it fails or is weak: A hot partition makes one consumer slower than the rest. Group coordinator Step What it does: Assigns partitions and tracks heartbeats. If it fails or is weak: If it is slow, rebalances take longer. Consumer 1 Step What it does: Reads partitions 0 to 2. If it fails or is weak: If it stalls, the group sees lag on its partitions. Consumer 2 Step What it does: Reads partitions 3 to 5. If it fails or is weak: A crash triggers a rebalance for the whole group. Committed offsets Data store What it does: The saved position for each partition. If it fails or is weak: Committing early loses records; late repeats them. Incident: Committed before it was done Incident Offsets were auto-committed while a batch was still processing, and a crash lost 800 records. $ kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe --group invoicer orders 0 CURRENT-OFFSET 5000 LOG-END-OFFSET 5000 LAG 0 $ psql -c \"select count(*) from invoices\" 4200 Lag showed zero, which looked healthy, but 800 records had been marked as read and never processed. The team moved to manual commits after the write and added a count check between topic and table. Common mistakes Leaving enable.auto.commit on with slow processing acks=1 for data that must not be lost A poll interval shorter than the processing time, causing endless rebalances Running more consumers than partitions"},"messaging/kafka-replication-isr-and-durability":{"html":"\u003cp>Durability in Kafka is not one switch but a combination of replication factor, acknowledgement level and minimum in-sync replicas. Knowing how they interact tells you exactly what a given failure costs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Leaders, followers and the in-sync set\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>replication.factor, min.insync.replicas and acks together\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unclean leader election and its trade-off\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rack awareness and safe maintenance\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"leaders-followers-and-the-isr\">Leaders, followers and the ISR\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Producers write to the partition \u003cstrong>leader\u003c/strong>; followers fetch from it. A follower that keeps up stays in the \u003cstrong>ISR\u003c/strong>. If the leader fails, the controller picks a new leader from the ISR, so no acknowledged record is lost. A follower that falls behind drops out of the ISR.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-three-settings\">The three settings\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Setting\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Common value\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>replication.factor\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Copies of each partition\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>min.insync.replicas\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Minimum ISR size to accept \u003ccode>acks=all\u003c/code> writes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>producer \u003ccode>acks\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>How many acknowledgements to wait for\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>all\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With 3 copies, \u003ccode>min.insync.replicas=2\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>acks=all\u003c/code>, one broker can fail with no data loss and no downtime. If a second fails, the ISR drops below 2 and writes are refused (the partition favours safety over availability). With \u003ccode>acks=1\u003c/code> a leader crash before followers copy can lose acknowledged records.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"unclean-election\">Unclean election\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If every ISR member is gone, the choices are to wait for one to return, or enable \u003ccode>unclean.leader.election.enable=true\u003c/code> and elect an out-of-sync replica. The second restores service quickly but \u003cstrong>discards records\u003c/strong> the replica never copied. Leave it off for data you cannot lose.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failure-domains-and-maintenance\">Failure domains and maintenance\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set \u003ccode>broker.rack\u003c/code> so replicas land in different racks or zones. For restarts use controlled shutdown, and roll one broker at a time, waiting until under-replicated partitions return to zero before moving on.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"prod&gt;lead;lead&gt;f1;lead&gt;f2;ctrl&gt;lead\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">What replication protects\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prod\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Producer acks=all\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lead\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Leader\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"f1\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Follower in ISR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"f2\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Follower out of sync\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctrl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Controller\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Producer acks=all to Leader; 2, Leader to Follower in ISR; 3, Leader to Follower out of sync; 4, Controller to Leader.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prod\">\u003cdt>Producer acks=all \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Waits until enough replicas have the record.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> acks=1 can lose a record if the leader dies at once.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lead\">\u003cdt>Leader \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Accepts writes and serves reads for the partition.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it fails, the controller elects a new one from the ISR.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"f1\">\u003cdt>Follower in ISR \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Caught up and eligible to become leader.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Falling behind removes it from the ISR.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"f2\">\u003cdt>Follower out of sync \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Lagging copy, not safe to elect.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Electing it (unclean) loses the missing records.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctrl\">\u003cdt>Controller \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Chooses leaders and tracks the ISR.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A controller election pauses leadership changes briefly.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-brokers-in-one-rack\">Incident: Two brokers in one rack\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A rack lost power and took two of three replicas of several partitions.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kafka-topics.sh --describe --under-min-isr-partitions\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kafka-topics.sh --describe --under-min-isr-partitions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Topic: orders Partition: 3 Leader: 2 Replicas: 1,2,3 Isr: 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">producer: NotEnoughReplicasException: Messages are rejected since there are fewer in-sync replicas than required\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The producers stopped rather than lose data, which was the intended behaviour. After power returned the followers caught up. The team added broker.rack so replicas would spread across racks, and tested a rack failure in a game day.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Replication factor 1 on important topics\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>acks=1 combined with a belief that data is safe\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enabling unclean election without understanding the loss\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restarting several brokers at once\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"leaders-followers-and-the-isr","text":"Leaders, followers and the ISR"},{"id":"the-three-settings","text":"The three settings"},{"id":"unclean-election","text":"Unclean election"},{"id":"failure-domains-and-maintenance","text":"Failure domains and maintenance"},{"id":"incident-two-brokers-in-one-rack","text":"Incident: Two brokers in one rack"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kafka replication","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#replication"},{"t":"Kafka broker configs","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#brokerconfigs"}],"text":"Durability in Kafka is not one switch but a combination of replication factor, acknowledgement level and minimum in-sync replicas. Knowing how they interact tells you exactly what a given failure costs. What you will learn Leaders, followers and the in-sync set replication.factor, min.insync.replicas and acks together Unclean leader election and its trade-off Rack awareness and safe maintenance Leaders, followers and the ISR Producers write to the partition leader ; followers fetch from it. A follower that keeps up stays in the ISR . If the leader fails, the controller picks a new leader from the ISR, so no acknowledged record is lost. A follower that falls behind drops out of the ISR. The three settings Setting Meaning Common value replication.factor Copies of each partition 3 min.insync.replicas Minimum ISR size to accept acks=all writes 2 producer acks How many acknowledgements to wait for all With 3 copies, min.insync.replicas=2 and acks=all , one broker can fail with no data loss and no downtime. If a second fails, the ISR drops below 2 and writes are refused (the partition favours safety over availability). With acks=1 a leader crash before followers copy can lose acknowledged records. Unclean election If every ISR member is gone, the choices are to wait for one to return, or enable unclean.leader.election.enable=true and elect an out-of-sync replica. The second restores service quickly but discards records the replica never copied. Leave it off for data you cannot lose. Failure domains and maintenance Set broker.rack so replicas land in different racks or zones. For restarts use controlled shutdown, and roll one broker at a time, waiting until under-replicated partitions return to zero before moving on. What replication protects Flow in order: 1, Producer acks=all to Leader; 2, Leader to Follower in ISR; 3, Leader to Follower out of sync; 4, Controller to Leader. Step Data store Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Producer acks=all Step What it does: Waits until enough replicas have the record. If it fails or is weak: acks=1 can lose a record if the leader dies at once. Leader Step What it does: Accepts writes and serves reads for the partition. If it fails or is weak: If it fails, the controller elects a new one from the ISR. Follower in ISR Data store What it does: Caught up and eligible to become leader. If it fails or is weak: Falling behind removes it from the ISR. Follower out of sync Data store What it does: Lagging copy, not safe to elect. If it fails or is weak: Electing it (unclean) loses the missing records. Controller Service What it does: Chooses leaders and tracks the ISR. If it fails or is weak: A controller election pauses leadership changes briefly. Incident: Two brokers in one rack Incident A rack lost power and took two of three replicas of several partitions. $ kafka-topics.sh --describe --under-min-isr-partitions Topic: orders Partition: 3 Leader: 2 Replicas: 1,2,3 Isr: 2 producer: NotEnoughReplicasException: Messages are rejected since there are fewer in-sync replicas than required The producers stopped rather than lose data, which was the intended behaviour. After power returned the followers caught up. The team added broker.rack so replicas would spread across racks, and tested a rack failure in a game day. Common mistakes Replication factor 1 on important topics acks=1 combined with a belief that data is safe Enabling unclean election without understanding the loss Restarting several brokers at once"},"messaging/kafka-retention-compaction-and-storage":{"html":"\u003cp>Kafka&#39;s cost is mostly disk. Retention is the lever: how many days of history, or how many bytes, to keep. Compaction is a different mode that turns a topic into a table of latest values. Choosing well saves money and avoids a full disk.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Time and size retention, and how segments are deleted\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Log compaction, keys and tombstones\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Estimating disk needs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tiered storage and long-term data\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deletion-retention\">Deletion retention\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kafka-configs.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --alter --entity-type topics --entity-name orders \\\n    --add-config retention.ms=604800000,retention.bytes=107374182400\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kafka-configs.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --alter --entity-type topics --entity-name orders \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --add-config retention.ms=604800000,retention.bytes=107374182400\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Kafka deletes data a \u003cstrong>segment\u003c/strong> at a time, not record by record. A segment is closed when it reaches \u003ccode>segment.bytes\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>segment.ms\u003c/code>; it becomes eligible for deletion when its newest record is older than \u003ccode>retention.ms\u003c/code>. The active segment is never deleted, so data can live longer than the setting suggests. \u003ccode>retention.bytes\u003c/code> applies per partition, not per topic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compaction\">Compaction\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With \u003ccode>cleanup.policy=compact\u003c/code>, Kafka keeps at least the latest record for every key and removes older ones in the background. The topic becomes a changelog of current state: user profiles, configuration, the latest price per product. A record with a key and a null value is a \u003cstrong>tombstone\u003c/strong> that deletes that key after \u003ccode>delete.retention.ms\u003c/code>. Compaction does not remove records from the active segment or guarantee a time, and consumers can still see older duplicates until cleaning runs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"estimating-disk\">Estimating disk\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Disk per broker is roughly: write rate × retention × replication factor ÷ number of brokers, plus 30 percent or more headroom. For 20 MB/s, 7 days and RF 3 across 6 brokers: 20 × 604,800 × 3 ÷ 6 ≈ 6 TB per broker before headroom.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tiered-storage\">Tiered storage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Newer Kafka versions can move old segments to cheaper object storage while keeping recent data on local disks, so you can keep long history without huge broker disks. Reads of old data are slower.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"write&gt;seg;seg&gt;ret;seg&gt;comp;seg&gt;alert\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Kafka frees disk\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"write\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Producers write\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"seg\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Closed segments\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ret\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Retention check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"comp\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Compaction\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"alert\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Disk alert\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Producers write to Closed segments; 2, Closed segments to Retention check; 3, Closed segments to Compaction; 4, Closed segments to Disk alert.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"write\">\u003cdt>Producers write \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Records append to the active segment.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A bug that doubles traffic doubles disk growth.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"seg\">\u003cdt>Closed segments \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A segment closes at its size or age limit.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The active segment is never deleted.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ret\">\u003cdt>Retention check \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Deletes segments older than retention.ms or beyond retention.bytes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Size limit above the free disk allows a full disk.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"comp\">\u003cdt>Compaction \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Keeps the latest record per key for compact topics.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Tombstones delete a key; do not use null for other things.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"alert\">\u003cdt>Disk alert \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Fires when free space is low.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Alerting at 95 percent leaves no time to react.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-full-disk-on-sunday\">Incident: The full disk on Sunday\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A producer bug doubled traffic, and retention kept more than the disks could hold.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"df -h /var/lib/kafka\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /var/lib/kafka\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/dev/nvme1n1  4.0T  4.0T  0  100%\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">kafka: java.io.IOException: No space left on device\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">kafka: Shutting down because log directory failed\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>After they freed space by lowering retention on the noisy topic, brokers recovered. The team set retention from real rates, added a disk alert at 70 percent and a quota on the misbehaving producer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Setting retention.bytes without noticing it is per partition\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting compaction to run immediately\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No disk alert or producer quota\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a compacted topic for events that should all be kept\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"deletion-retention","text":"Deletion retention"},{"id":"compaction","text":"Compaction"},{"id":"estimating-disk","text":"Estimating disk"},{"id":"tiered-storage","text":"Tiered storage"},{"id":"incident-the-full-disk-on-sunday","text":"Incident: The full disk on Sunday"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kafka log compaction","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#compaction"},{"t":"Kafka topic configs","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#topicconfigs"}],"text":"Kafka&#39;s cost is mostly disk. Retention is the lever: how many days of history, or how many bytes, to keep. Compaction is a different mode that turns a topic into a table of latest values. Choosing well saves money and avoids a full disk. What you will learn Time and size retention, and how segments are deleted Log compaction, keys and tombstones Estimating disk needs Tiered storage and long-term data Deletion retention $ kafka-configs.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --alter --entity-type topics --entity-name orders \\ --add-config retention.ms=604800000,retention.bytes=107374182400 Kafka deletes data a segment at a time, not record by record. A segment is closed when it reaches segment.bytes or segment.ms ; it becomes eligible for deletion when its newest record is older than retention.ms . The active segment is never deleted, so data can live longer than the setting suggests. retention.bytes applies per partition, not per topic. Compaction With cleanup.policy=compact , Kafka keeps at least the latest record for every key and removes older ones in the background. The topic becomes a changelog of current state: user profiles, configuration, the latest price per product. A record with a key and a null value is a tombstone that deletes that key after delete.retention.ms . Compaction does not remove records from the active segment or guarantee a time, and consumers can still see older duplicates until cleaning runs. Estimating disk Disk per broker is roughly: write rate × retention × replication factor ÷ number of brokers, plus 30 percent or more headroom. For 20 MB/s, 7 days and RF 3 across 6 brokers: 20 × 604,800 × 3 ÷ 6 ≈ 6 TB per broker before headroom. Tiered storage Newer Kafka versions can move old segments to cheaper object storage while keeping recent data on local disks, so you can keep long history without huge broker disks. Reads of old data are slower. How Kafka frees disk Flow in order: 1, Producers write to Closed segments; 2, Closed segments to Retention check; 3, Closed segments to Compaction; 4, Closed segments to Disk alert. Step Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Producers write Step What it does: Records append to the active segment. If it fails or is weak: A bug that doubles traffic doubles disk growth. Closed segments Data store What it does: A segment closes at its size or age limit. If it fails or is weak: The active segment is never deleted. Retention check Step What it does: Deletes segments older than retention.ms or beyond retention.bytes. If it fails or is weak: Size limit above the free disk allows a full disk. Compaction Step What it does: Keeps the latest record per key for compact topics. If it fails or is weak: Tombstones delete a key; do not use null for other things. Disk alert Observability What it does: Fires when free space is low. If it fails or is weak: Alerting at 95 percent leaves no time to react. Incident: The full disk on Sunday Incident A producer bug doubled traffic, and retention kept more than the disks could hold. $ df -h /var/lib/kafka /dev/nvme1n1 4.0T 4.0T 0 100% kafka: java.io.IOException: No space left on device kafka: Shutting down because log directory failed After they freed space by lowering retention on the noisy topic, brokers recovered. The team set retention from real rates, added a disk alert at 70 percent and a quota on the misbehaving producer. Common mistakes Setting retention.bytes without noticing it is per partition Expecting compaction to run immediately No disk alert or producer quota Using a compacted topic for events that should all be kept"},"messaging/kafka-lag-rebalances-and-capacity-in-operation":{"html":"\u003cp>A Kafka cluster that works on day one needs watching on day 100. The same few signals reveal almost every trouble: consumers behind, replicas out of sync, disks filling, and a controller that keeps changing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Consumer lag as records and as time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Broker health metrics that matter\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A decision path for lag and rebalance incidents\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capacity planning and safe rolling changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"consumer-lag\">Consumer lag\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Lag\u003c/strong> is the difference between the newest offset in a partition and the consumer&#39;s committed offset.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --describe --group invoicer\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --describe --group invoicer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">TOPIC   PARTITION  CURRENT-OFFSET  LOG-END-OFFSET  LAG    CONSUMER-ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">orders  0          1,200,000       1,200,040       40     c-1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">orders  1          900,000         1,250,000       350000 c-2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>One partition far behind points to a slow or stuck consumer or a hot key; all partitions behind points to a consumer group that is too slow overall or a producer burst. Convert lag to time: lag divided by the consumption rate. &quot;12 minutes behind&quot; is easier to alert on than &quot;350,000&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"broker-signals\">Broker signals\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Metric\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Healthy\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Problem when\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Under-replicated partitions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Above 0 for minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Offline partitions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Above 0\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Active controller count\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0 or changing often\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>ISR shrinks and expands\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Rare\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Frequent: slow disks or network\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Request latency (produce, fetch)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Low and steady\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Rising\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Disk used, network utilisation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Well below limits\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Near limits\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deciding-what-to-do\">Deciding what to do\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Consumers alive?\u003c/strong> Check the group state and logs. A crashed consumer or a rebalance loop is more common than a cluster problem.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Processing slow?\u003c/strong> Look at the downstream database or API the consumer calls.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Hot partition?\u003c/strong> Check key distribution.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Under capacity?\u003c/strong> Add consumers up to the partition count; add partitions only with a plan for the key change.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Broker trouble?\u003c/strong> Check disks, network and ISR changes.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"alert&gt;alive;alive&gt;slow;slow&gt;hot;hot&gt;act\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Lag incident decision path\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"alert\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Lag alert\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"alive\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Are consumers alive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"slow\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Is processing slow\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"hot\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Hot partition or broker\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"act\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scale or fix\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Lag alert to Are consumers alive; 2, Are consumers alive to Is processing slow; 3, Is processing slow to Hot partition or broker; 4, Hot partition or broker to Scale or fix.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"alert\">\u003cdt>Lag alert \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Time behind is above the limit and rising.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A static record-count threshold misleads when traffic changes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"alive\">\u003cdt>Are consumers alive \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Check group state, errors and rebalances.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A rebalance loop looks like lag but is a configuration problem.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"slow\">\u003cdt>Is processing slow \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Look at downstream calls and per-record time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Adding consumers does not help if the database is the limit.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"hot\">\u003cdt>Hot partition or broker \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Check key spread, disk and network on brokers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One broker with a slow disk drags all its partitions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"act\">\u003cdt>Scale or fix \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Scale consumers, fix code, rebalance keys or add brokers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Changing partitions mid-incident can break ordering.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity-and-change\">Capacity and change\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep disks below about 70 percent, network below 60, and enough headroom to lose one broker and still serve. Roll changes one broker at a time, waiting for under-replicated partitions to reach zero. Test the full restart procedure in a staging cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-rebalance-loop\">Incident: The rebalance loop\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A consumer group rebalanced every few minutes and lag grew, though the cluster was healthy.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">consumer: Member c-2 sending LeaveGroup request: consumer poll timeout has expired\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">coordinator: Preparing to rebalance group invoicer (generation 412)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">lag (time behind): 18 minutes and growing\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Batches had grown and took longer than max.poll.interval.ms, so the coordinator removed the consumer each time. Lowering max.poll.records and raising the interval ended the loop, and the alert now shows minutes behind.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Alerting on a fixed lag number\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding partitions before checking whether consumers are alive\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting disks run above 80 percent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restarting brokers quickly without waiting for replicas to catch up\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"consumer-lag","text":"Consumer lag"},{"id":"broker-signals","text":"Broker signals"},{"id":"deciding-what-to-do","text":"Deciding what to do"},{"id":"capacity-and-change","text":"Capacity and change"},{"id":"incident-the-rebalance-loop","text":"Incident: The rebalance loop"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kafka monitoring","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#monitoring"},{"t":"Kafka operations","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#operations"}],"text":"A Kafka cluster that works on day one needs watching on day 100. The same few signals reveal almost every trouble: consumers behind, replicas out of sync, disks filling, and a controller that keeps changing. What you will learn Consumer lag as records and as time Broker health metrics that matter A decision path for lag and rebalance incidents Capacity planning and safe rolling changes Consumer lag Lag is the difference between the newest offset in a partition and the consumer&#39;s committed offset. $ kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server b1:9092 --describe --group invoicer TOPIC PARTITION CURRENT-OFFSET LOG-END-OFFSET LAG CONSUMER-ID orders 0 1,200,000 1,200,040 40 c-1 orders 1 900,000 1,250,000 350000 c-2 One partition far behind points to a slow or stuck consumer or a hot key; all partitions behind points to a consumer group that is too slow overall or a producer burst. Convert lag to time: lag divided by the consumption rate. \"12 minutes behind\" is easier to alert on than \"350,000\". Broker signals Metric Healthy Problem when Under-replicated partitions 0 Above 0 for minutes Offline partitions 0 Above 0 Active controller count 1 0 or changing often ISR shrinks and expands Rare Frequent: slow disks or network Request latency (produce, fetch) Low and steady Rising Disk used, network utilisation Well below limits Near limits Deciding what to do Consumers alive? Check the group state and logs. A crashed consumer or a rebalance loop is more common than a cluster problem. Processing slow? Look at the downstream database or API the consumer calls. Hot partition? Check key distribution. Under capacity? Add consumers up to the partition count; add partitions only with a plan for the key change. Broker trouble? Check disks, network and ISR changes. Lag incident decision path Flow in order: 1, Lag alert to Are consumers alive; 2, Are consumers alive to Is processing slow; 3, Is processing slow to Hot partition or broker; 4, Hot partition or broker to Scale or fix. Observability Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Lag alert Observability What it does: Time behind is above the limit and rising. If it fails or is weak: A static record-count threshold misleads when traffic changes. Are consumers alive Step What it does: Check group state, errors and rebalances. If it fails or is weak: A rebalance loop looks like lag but is a configuration problem. Is processing slow Step What it does: Look at downstream calls and per-record time. If it fails or is weak: Adding consumers does not help if the database is the limit. Hot partition or broker Data store What it does: Check key spread, disk and network on brokers. If it fails or is weak: One broker with a slow disk drags all its partitions. Scale or fix Step What it does: Scale consumers, fix code, rebalance keys or add brokers. If it fails or is weak: Changing partitions mid-incident can break ordering. Capacity and change Keep disks below about 70 percent, network below 60, and enough headroom to lose one broker and still serve. Roll changes one broker at a time, waiting for under-replicated partitions to reach zero. Test the full restart procedure in a staging cluster. Incident: The rebalance loop Incident A consumer group rebalanced every few minutes and lag grew, though the cluster was healthy. consumer: Member c-2 sending LeaveGroup request: consumer poll timeout has expired coordinator: Preparing to rebalance group invoicer (generation 412) lag (time behind): 18 minutes and growing Batches had grown and took longer than max.poll.interval.ms, so the coordinator removed the consumer each time. Lowering max.poll.records and raising the interval ended the loop, and the alert now shows minutes behind. Common mistakes Alerting on a fixed lag number Adding partitions before checking whether consumers are alive Letting disks run above 80 percent Restarting brokers quickly without waiting for repli"},"messaging/managed-messaging-on-cloud-sqs-sns-pub-sub-and-event-hubs":{"html":"\u003cp>Every major cloud offers messaging as a service. They look similar from far away and differ in ordering, retention, replay and price. Picking from the map of equivalents, then checking the limits that matter to you, keeps both cost and operating work low.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Queue, topic and stream services on each cloud\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ordering, retention, replay and delivery per service\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dead-letter queues and visibility timeouts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Managed Kafka and when to run your own\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-map\">The map\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Need\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>AWS\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Google Cloud\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Azure\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Work queue\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>SQS\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pub/Sub (pull subscription)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Service Bus queue\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Fan-out topic\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>SNS\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pub/Sub topic\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Service Bus topic\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Partitioned log, replay\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Kinesis\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pub/Sub with seek, or Managed Kafka\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Event Hubs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Managed Kafka\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MSK\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Managed Service for Apache Kafka\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Event Hubs Kafka endpoint\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"things-that-differ\">Things that differ\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Ordering:\u003c/strong> SQS standard is best-effort ordered; FIFO queues order by message group id with lower throughput. Pub/Sub offers ordering keys. Service Bus uses sessions. Event Hubs and Kinesis order within a partition or shard.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Delivery:\u003c/strong> SQS standard, SNS and Pub/Sub are at-least-once, so consumers must be idempotent. FIFO queues can deduplicate within a window.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Retention and replay:\u003c/strong> queues delete after handling; logs and Pub/Sub (with retained messages and seek) allow replay for a set time.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Failure handling:\u003c/strong> use \u003cstrong>dead-letter queues\u003c/strong> with a maximum receive count, and set the \u003cstrong>visibility timeout\u003c/strong> (SQS) or acknowledgement deadline longer than processing time, otherwise a message is delivered to a second consumer while the first is still working.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cost:\u003c/strong> most services charge per request or per throughput unit; many small messages can cost more than one large batch.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fan-out-pattern\">Fan-out pattern\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A common design: publish once to a topic (SNS or Pub/Sub), and attach a queue per consumer so each system reads at its own speed with its own dead-letter queue.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"svc&gt;topic;topic&gt;qa;topic&gt;qb;qa&gt;dlq\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Fan-out with managed services\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svc\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Producer service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"topic\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Topic\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"qa\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Billing queue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"qb\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Search queue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dlq\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Dead-letter queue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Producer service to Topic; 2, Topic to Billing queue; 3, Topic to Search queue; 4, Billing queue to Dead-letter queue.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svc\">\u003cdt>Producer service \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Publishes an event once to a topic.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Large messages hit size limits; send a pointer to data instead.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"topic\">\u003cdt>Topic \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> SNS or Pub/Sub topic that copies the event to subscriptions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A missing subscription means that consumer misses events.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"qa\">\u003cdt>Billing queue \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Its own queue and dead-letter queue.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Visibility timeout shorter than the work causes duplicates.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"qb\">\u003cdt>Search queue \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Independent speed and retry policy.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An unwatched dead-letter queue hides failures.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dlq\">\u003cdt>Dead-letter queue \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Collects messages that exceed the receive count.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without an alert it silently fills.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"run-it-yourself-or-rent-it\">Run it yourself or rent it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run Kafka yourself when you need its ecosystem, very high throughput, long replay or low cost at large scale, and you have people to operate it. Otherwise choose the managed service closest to your cloud. Wrap the client in a small interface so a later move is possible.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-duplicates-after-a-slow-job\">Incident: Duplicates after a slow job\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A queue message was delivered twice because processing took longer than the visibility timeout.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws sqs get-queue-attributes --queue-url $Q --attribute-names VisibilityTimeout\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws sqs get-queue-attributes --queue-url \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$Q\u003c/span> --attribute-names VisibilityTimeout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">VisibilityTimeout: 30\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">worker log: job 8812 took 95s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">worker log: message received 3 times\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The message became visible again after 30 seconds and another worker picked it up. The team raised the timeout above the longest job, extended it during long jobs, added a dead-letter queue and made the job idempotent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A visibility timeout shorter than the longest job\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No dead-letter queue or no alert on it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating standard queues as ordered\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing self-managed Kafka for a small workload\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-map","text":"The map"},{"id":"things-that-differ","text":"Things that differ"},{"id":"fan-out-pattern","text":"Fan-out pattern"},{"id":"run-it-yourself-or-rent-it","text":"Run it yourself or rent it"},{"id":"incident-duplicates-after-a-slow-job","text":"Incident: Duplicates after a slow job"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Amazon SQS and SNS","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/welcome.html"},{"t":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub","u":"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview"},{"t":"Azure Service Bus","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/service-bus-messaging/service-bus-messaging-overview"},{"t":"Azure Event Hubs","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/event-hubs/event-hubs-about"}],"text":"Every major cloud offers messaging as a service. They look similar from far away and differ in ordering, retention, replay and price. Picking from the map of equivalents, then checking the limits that matter to you, keeps both cost and operating work low. What you will learn Queue, topic and stream services on each cloud Ordering, retention, replay and delivery per service Dead-letter queues and visibility timeouts Managed Kafka and when to run your own The map Need AWS Google Cloud Azure Work queue SQS Pub/Sub (pull subscription) Service Bus queue Fan-out topic SNS Pub/Sub topic Service Bus topic Partitioned log, replay Kinesis Pub/Sub with seek, or Managed Kafka Event Hubs Managed Kafka MSK Managed Service for Apache Kafka Event Hubs Kafka endpoint Things that differ Ordering: SQS standard is best-effort ordered; FIFO queues order by message group id with lower throughput. Pub/Sub offers ordering keys. Service Bus uses sessions. Event Hubs and Kinesis order within a partition or shard. Delivery: SQS standard, SNS and Pub/Sub are at-least-once, so consumers must be idempotent. FIFO queues can deduplicate within a window. Retention and replay: queues delete after handling; logs and Pub/Sub (with retained messages and seek) allow replay for a set time. Failure handling: use dead-letter queues with a maximum receive count, and set the visibility timeout (SQS) or acknowledgement deadline longer than processing time, otherwise a message is delivered to a second consumer while the first is still working. Cost: most services charge per request or per throughput unit; many small messages can cost more than one large batch. Fan-out pattern A common design: publish once to a topic (SNS or Pub/Sub), and attach a queue per consumer so each system reads at its own speed with its own dead-letter queue. Fan-out with managed services Flow in order: 1, Producer service to Topic; 2, Topic to Billing queue; 3, Topic to Search queue; 4, Billing queue to Dead-letter queue. Service Queue Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Producer service Service What it does: Publishes an event once to a topic. If it fails or is weak: Large messages hit size limits; send a pointer to data instead. Topic Queue What it does: SNS or Pub/Sub topic that copies the event to subscriptions. If it fails or is weak: A missing subscription means that consumer misses events. Billing queue Queue What it does: Its own queue and dead-letter queue. If it fails or is weak: Visibility timeout shorter than the work causes duplicates. Search queue Queue What it does: Independent speed and retry policy. If it fails or is weak: An unwatched dead-letter queue hides failures. Dead-letter queue Observability What it does: Collects messages that exceed the receive count. If it fails or is weak: Without an alert it silently fills. Run it yourself or rent it Run Kafka yourself when you need its ecosystem, very high throughput, long replay or low cost at large scale, and you have people to operate it. Otherwise choose the managed service closest to your cloud. Wrap the client in a small interface so a later move is possible. Incident: Duplicates after a slow job Incident A queue message was delivered twice because processing took longer than the visibility timeout. $ aws sqs get-queue-attributes --queue-url $Q --attribute-names VisibilityTimeout VisibilityTimeout: 30 worker log: job 8812 took 95s worker log: message received 3 times The message became visible again after 30 seconds and another worker picked it up. The team raised the timeout above the longest job, extended it during long jobs, added a dead-letter queue and made the job idempotent. Common mistakes A visibility timeout shorter than the longest job No dead-letter queue or no alert on it Treating standard queues as ordered Choosing self-managed Kafka for a small workload"},"messaging/schemas-contracts-and-evolving-messages":{"html":"\u003cp>Messages outlive code. A topic can hold a week or a year of records, and consumers written by other teams read them at different times. A schema and compatibility rules turn a hopeful agreement into something a build can enforce.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why a schema beats unstructured JSON\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backward, forward and full compatibility\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe and unsafe changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Registry, subjects and CI checks\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"schema-formats\">Schema formats\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>JSON Schema\u003c/strong> describes JSON, easy to read, larger on the wire. \u003cstrong>Avro\u003c/strong> stores data in compact binary and the schema travels by id; it supports defaults and evolution. \u003cstrong>Protobuf\u003c/strong> uses numbered fields, so names can change but numbers must not.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{\n  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;record&quot;, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;OrderPaid&quot;, &quot;namespace&quot;: &quot;shop&quot;,\n  &quot;fields&quot;: [\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;id&quot;, &quot;type&quot;: &quot;string&quot;},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;total&quot;, &quot;type&quot;: &quot;double&quot;},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;currency&quot;, &quot;type&quot;: &quot;string&quot;, &quot;default&quot;: &quot;EUR&quot;}\n  ]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;record&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;OrderPaid&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;namespace&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;fields&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;string&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;total&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;double&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;currency&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;string&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;default&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;EUR&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compatibility\">Compatibility\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Mode\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Upgrade order\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Backward\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>New schema can read old data\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Consumers first\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Forward\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Old schema can read new data\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Producers first\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Full\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Both\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Any order\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safe-changes\">Safe changes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Add a field \u003cstrong>with a default\u003c/strong> (safe for backward compatibility).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Remove an \u003cstrong>optional\u003c/strong> field.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never change a field&#39;s type, reuse a field name or Protobuf field number, or make an optional field required.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For a breaking change, \u003cstrong>expand then contract\u003c/strong>: add the new field, write both, move all consumers, then remove the old one later.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"registry\">Registry\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>schema registry\u003c/strong> stores versions under a \u003cstrong>subject\u003c/strong> (usually topic name plus key or value). Producers register or look up a schema and send only its id. The registry rejects a new version that breaks the chosen compatibility mode. Run the compatibility check in CI so an incompatible change fails the pull request.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"dev&gt;ci;ci&gt;reg;reg&gt;prod;prod&gt;cons\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How a schema change is checked\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dev\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Developer changes schema\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ci\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CI compatibility check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"reg\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Schema registry\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prod\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Producer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cons\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Consumers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Developer changes schema to CI compatibility check; 2, CI compatibility check to Schema registry; 3, Schema registry to Producer; 4, Producer to Consumers.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dev\">\u003cdt>Developer changes schema \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Adds a field or changes a type in the schema file.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A rename looks harmless but breaks readers.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ci\">\u003cdt>CI compatibility check \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Asks the registry whether the new version is compatible.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping CI lets a breaking change reach production.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"reg\">\u003cdt>Schema registry \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stores versions per subject and enforces the mode.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it is down, producers may be unable to send.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prod\">\u003cdt>Producer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Writes records with the schema id.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An old producer keeps writing the old format for a while.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cons\">\u003cdt>Consumers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Look up the schema by id to decode records.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A consumer on an incompatible schema fails on old records.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-revenue-of-zero\">Incident: Revenue of zero\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A producer renamed a field and consumers read nulls for a day.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;select sum(amount) from daily_revenue where day = current_date - 1&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select sum(amount) from daily_revenue where day = current_date - 1&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0.00\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">producer: schema v7 renamed amount to total\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">consumer: schema v6 expects amount, got null\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The rename was not a compatible change. The team reverted, replayed the day from the topic, and enabled a full-compatibility check in CI. Renames now happen by adding the new field and removing the old one later.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Renaming or retyping a field in place\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Required fields with no default\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reusing a Protobuf field number\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No compatibility check in the build\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"schema-formats","text":"Schema formats"},{"id":"compatibility","text":"Compatibility"},{"id":"safe-changes","text":"Safe changes"},{"id":"registry","text":"Registry"},{"id":"incident-revenue-of-zero","text":"Incident: Revenue of zero"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Confluent schema evolution and compatibility","u":"https://docs.confluent.io/platform/current/schema-registry/fundamentals/schema-evolution.html"},{"t":"Protocol Buffers: updating a message type","u":"https://protobuf.dev/programming-guides/proto3/#updating"}],"text":"Messages outlive code. A topic can hold a week or a year of records, and consumers written by other teams read them at different times. A schema and compatibility rules turn a hopeful agreement into something a build can enforce. What you will learn Why a schema beats unstructured JSON Backward, forward and full compatibility Safe and unsafe changes Registry, subjects and CI checks Schema formats JSON Schema describes JSON, easy to read, larger on the wire. Avro stores data in compact binary and the schema travels by id; it supports defaults and evolution. Protobuf uses numbered fields, so names can change but numbers must not. { \"type\" : \"record\" , \"name\" : \"OrderPaid\" , \"namespace\" : \"shop\" , \"fields\" : [ { \"name\" : \"id\" , \"type\" : \"string\" } , { \"name\" : \"total\" , \"type\" : \"double\" } , { \"name\" : \"currency\" , \"type\" : \"string\" , \"default\" : \"EUR\" } ] } Compatibility Mode Meaning Upgrade order Backward New schema can read old data Consumers first Forward Old schema can read new data Producers first Full Both Any order Safe changes Add a field with a default (safe for backward compatibility). Remove an optional field. Never change a field&#39;s type, reuse a field name or Protobuf field number, or make an optional field required. For a breaking change, expand then contract : add the new field, write both, move all consumers, then remove the old one later. Registry A schema registry stores versions under a subject (usually topic name plus key or value). Producers register or look up a schema and send only its id. The registry rejects a new version that breaks the chosen compatibility mode. Run the compatibility check in CI so an incompatible change fails the pull request. How a schema change is checked Flow in order: 1, Developer changes schema to CI compatibility check; 2, CI compatibility check to Schema registry; 3, Schema registry to Producer; 4, Producer to Consumers. Client Observability Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Developer changes schema Client What it does: Adds a field or changes a type in the schema file. If it fails or is weak: A rename looks harmless but breaks readers. CI compatibility check Observability What it does: Asks the registry whether the new version is compatible. If it fails or is weak: Skipping CI lets a breaking change reach production. Schema registry Data store What it does: Stores versions per subject and enforces the mode. If it fails or is weak: If it is down, producers may be unable to send. Producer Step What it does: Writes records with the schema id. If it fails or is weak: An old producer keeps writing the old format for a while. Consumers Step What it does: Look up the schema by id to decode records. If it fails or is weak: A consumer on an incompatible schema fails on old records. Incident: Revenue of zero Incident A producer renamed a field and consumers read nulls for a day. $ psql -c \"select sum(amount) from daily_revenue where day = current_date - 1\" 0.00 producer: schema v7 renamed amount to total consumer: schema v6 expects amount, got null The rename was not a compatible change. The team reverted, replayed the day from the topic, and enabled a full-compatibility check in CI. Renames now happen by adding the new field and removing the old one later. Common mistakes Renaming or retyping a field in place Required fields with no default Reusing a Protobuf field number No compatibility check in the build"},"messaging/debugging-messaging-incidents":{"html":"\u003cp>When a message system misbehaves, the symptom is rarely where the cause is. A queue that grows is a consumer problem; missing data may be a routing or retention problem; duplicates are normal until they are not handled. A checklist per symptom keeps you from changing the wrong thing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A checklist for backlog and lag\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A checklist for lost, duplicated and out-of-order messages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tracing one message end to end\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What to put in dashboards and alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"backlog-or-lag-is-growing\">Backlog or lag is growing\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Are consumers running and connected? (consumer count, group state)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is each consumer making progress? (processing time, errors, rebalance loop)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Did the producer rate jump? (publish rate, a retry storm)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is the work stuck on a dependency? (database, API, lock)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is it one partition or queue? (key skew, one broker)\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"messages-are-missing\">Messages are missing\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Was the message accepted? (publisher confirm, \u003ccode>acks\u003c/code>, producer errors)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Was it routed? (binding, topic and subscription filters, exchange alternate)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is it still retained? (retention time, queue TTL, compaction)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Was it acknowledged too early? (auto-ack, commit before processing)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Did a failover lose it? (acks=1, non-durable queue, unclean election)\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"duplicates-and-ordering\">Duplicates and ordering\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Duplicates come from redelivery after a crash or timeout, producer retries and rebalances. The cure is an idempotent consumer, not trying to eliminate them. Out-of-order arrival comes from several partitions or queues, concurrent consumers and retries. Cure by key, one consumer per partition, or sequence numbers checked by the consumer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"trace-one-message\">Trace one message\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every message needs a stable \u003cstrong>id\u003c/strong> that appears in producer logs, broker headers, consumer logs and the database write. With it, one search shows where the message stopped. Add the id to your trace and log format from the start.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"prod&gt;broker;broker&gt;cons;cons&gt;db;cons&gt;dlq\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Following a message\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prod\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Producer log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"broker\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Broker metrics\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cons\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Consumer log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"db\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Database effect\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dlq\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Dead letters\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Producer log to Broker metrics; 2, Broker metrics to Consumer log; 3, Consumer log to Database effect; 4, Consumer log to Dead letters.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prod\">\u003cdt>Producer log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Logs the message id and confirmation.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing confirmations mean it may never have been stored.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"broker\">\u003cdt>Broker metrics \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shows accepted, routed, unroutable and retained counts.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An exchange with no binding drops messages silently.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cons\">\u003cdt>Consumer log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Logs receipt, success, failure and ack of the id.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No log line for the id means it never arrived.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"db\">\u003cdt>Database effect \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The row or call that proves the work happened.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A row without a log line means the logs are incomplete.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dlq\">\u003cdt>Dead letters \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Collects messages that failed repeatedly.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unwatched dead letters hide the problem.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-orders-that-vanished\">Incident: The orders that vanished\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Some orders never appeared in reports, and nobody could say where they stopped.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">producer.log: published order=7731 key=order.created.us\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">consumer.log: (no entries for order=7731)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">broker: exchange events unroutable_dropped 212\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Orders for one region used a key no binding matched. Following one id showed it stopped at the exchange. The team added a binding, an alternate exchange for unroutable messages and an alert on dropped count.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Searching consumer code before checking whether the broker accepted the message\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging without a message id\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating duplicates as a bug in the broker\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the dead-letter queue until a customer complains\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"backlog-or-lag-is-growing","text":"Backlog or lag is growing"},{"id":"messages-are-missing","text":"Messages are missing"},{"id":"duplicates-and-ordering","text":"Duplicates and ordering"},{"id":"trace-one-message","text":"Trace one message"},{"id":"incident-the-orders-that-vanished","text":"Incident: The orders that vanished"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"RabbitMQ troubleshooting","u":"https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/troubleshooting"},{"t":"Kafka operations: troubleshooting","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#operations"}],"text":"When a message system misbehaves, the symptom is rarely where the cause is. A queue that grows is a consumer problem; missing data may be a routing or retention problem; duplicates are normal until they are not handled. A checklist per symptom keeps you from changing the wrong thing. What you will learn A checklist for backlog and lag A checklist for lost, duplicated and out-of-order messages Tracing one message end to end What to put in dashboards and alerts Backlog or lag is growing Are consumers running and connected? (consumer count, group state) Is each consumer making progress? (processing time, errors, rebalance loop) Did the producer rate jump? (publish rate, a retry storm) Is the work stuck on a dependency? (database, API, lock) Is it one partition or queue? (key skew, one broker) Messages are missing Was the message accepted? (publisher confirm, acks , producer errors) Was it routed? (binding, topic and subscription filters, exchange alternate) Is it still retained? (retention time, queue TTL, compaction) Was it acknowledged too early? (auto-ack, commit before processing) Did a failover lose it? (acks=1, non-durable queue, unclean election) Duplicates and ordering Duplicates come from redelivery after a crash or timeout, producer retries and rebalances. The cure is an idempotent consumer, not trying to eliminate them. Out-of-order arrival comes from several partitions or queues, concurrent consumers and retries. Cure by key, one consumer per partition, or sequence numbers checked by the consumer. Trace one message Every message needs a stable id that appears in producer logs, broker headers, consumer logs and the database write. With it, one search shows where the message stopped. Add the id to your trace and log format from the start. Following a message Flow in order: 1, Producer log to Broker metrics; 2, Broker metrics to Consumer log; 3, Consumer log to Database effect; 4, Consumer log to Dead letters. Observability Queue Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Producer log Observability What it does: Logs the message id and confirmation. If it fails or is weak: Missing confirmations mean it may never have been stored. Broker metrics Queue What it does: Shows accepted, routed, unroutable and retained counts. If it fails or is weak: An exchange with no binding drops messages silently. Consumer log Observability What it does: Logs receipt, success, failure and ack of the id. If it fails or is weak: No log line for the id means it never arrived. Database effect Data store What it does: The row or call that proves the work happened. If it fails or is weak: A row without a log line means the logs are incomplete. Dead letters Observability What it does: Collects messages that failed repeatedly. If it fails or is weak: Unwatched dead letters hide the problem. Incident: The orders that vanished Incident Some orders never appeared in reports, and nobody could say where they stopped. producer.log: published order=7731 key=order.created.us consumer.log: (no entries for order=7731) broker: exchange events unroutable_dropped 212 Orders for one region used a key no binding matched. Following one id showed it stopped at the exchange. The team added a binding, an alternate exchange for unroutable messages and an alert on dropped count. Common mistakes Searching consumer code before checking whether the broker accepted the message Logging without a message id Treating duplicates as a bug in the broker Ignoring the dead-letter queue until a customer complains"}});